{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["A linear projection is a mathematically defined transformation, while a checklist is an active cognitive process subject to interpretation and fatigue.","Expert intuition may rely on non-linear feature interactions that a strictly linear checklist projection cannot represent.","The Condorcet theorem assumes independent errors, but a shared checklist forces perfectly correlated errors if the checklist itself is flawed."],"overturn_conditions":["Empirical evidence shows that checklists inherently capture non-linear feature interactions.","It is demonstrated that high inter-member agreement is a reliable signal of genuine mechanistic understanding rather than proxy-based compliance.","A panel using a checklist based on flawed proxy features consistently outperforms the expert at the decision boundary."],"shared_structure":["Both an expert's intuition and a linear projection of that expert seek to approximate a true decision boundary.","In both single-expert and panel settings, proxy features (checklist items) are used to substitute for direct observation of the generative mechanism.","Aggregating multiple low-fidelity projections can yield a lower mean squared error than a single high-fidelity projection, provided the errors are sufficiently independent."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_001"],"transfer_claims":["The checklist functions as a lossy linear projection of the expert's high-dimensional nonlinear intuition.","Panel aggregation reduces the variance of the projection error, but cannot eliminate the bias introduced by the projection itself.","Evaluating individual panel member variance reveals whether the checklist preserves necessary information or merely forces mechanical compliance."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"For tasks with an objectively verifiable outcome, the reliability of a non-expert panel is conditional on the checklist representing a sufficient decomposition of the expert's implicit causal model. If it is merely a correlated proxy (capturing surface heuristics rather than the generative mechanism), the panel will show in-distribution compliance but will fail to outperform the expert at the decision boundary, where intuition integrates unstated contextual cues. Within a single evaluation, genuine reliability gain is distinguished from compliance by the variance of panel members' individual judgments: a checklist that merely tracks the right answer will force high inter-member agreement by replacing judgment with mechanical rule-following, whereas a genuine reliability gain will preserve high individual variance that is subsequently averaged out by aggregation.","counterconditions":["The expert's intuition is actually based on the exact same surface heuristics as the checklist, making the checklist a perfect proxy rather than a lossy projection.","The objective outcome space is dominated by adversarial cases where the causal model is non-decomposable, rendering any checklist inherently unstable.","Panel members communicate or influence each other prior to judgment, destroying the independence required for their individual variance to average out."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Checklists operate by decomposing a complex judgment into additive, verifiable sub-checks. If the checklist successfully encodes the causal graph of the domain, the panel's aggregated probability of error decays exponentially with panel size (via a Condorcet-style mechanism). However, if the checklist only encodes epiphenomenal correlates, it acts as a lossy linear projection of a nonlinear expert boundary. The panel's error rate will then plateau at the Bayes error rate of the proxy features. The distinguishing evidence relies on the fact that proxy-based compliance artificially suppresses individual judgment variance by forcing rule-following, whereas genuine mechanistic understanding allows diverse exploration of the feature space that averages out in the aggregate.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.75}],"simulation_proposals":[]}